ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.4.md:529:**Why one buffer set, not two generations (Claude review R1).** In the canonical loop, step t+1 needs actions from the forward on obs t, and that forward consumes the H2D copy of obs t. A second generation therefore buys no overlap. It would cost a second pinned buffer set, a full-row copy on every truncate, a new trainer API at every copy site, and a change to Isaiah's truncate plumbing (:991). The contract allows a synchronous baseline ("A synchronous-copy baseline satisfies this", W:docs/kaggriculture-contract.md, Environment). R1 makes that baseline explicit with the entry fence. Reopen double buffering only if a measured profile shows native step overlapping with device work would pay (Phase 6 Nsight), and then with the event design Codex drafted (preserved in commit `6ea11d2`, Codex's draft of this brief).
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.4.md:531:Fence tests: (1) spy test: for `reset`, `step` and `truncate_envs`, `synchronize` is called before the native write, and a failed (spy) native call leaves all 35 buffers byte-identical. That proves only that the adapter adds no write on error; native atomicity is proven by Task D's native rollback tests, never by adapter spies; (2) CPU/unpinned path makes zero CUDA calls; (3) **pod-only DMA test** (runs in the early smoke or 6.1 receipt, not on the Mac): pinned buffers, enqueue a nonblocking H2D copy of every buffer behind a long device kernel, call `step`, then compare the device copy with a pre-step host clone. It fails if the fence is removed. That test is the L6 evidence; the spy test only proves ordering. This prevents host reuse hazards; **Task 0.2 was an Inductor GEMM overflow**, not this bug (W:cookbook/references/compiled-gemm-template-overflows-above-2-21-rows.md:16).
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.4.md:541:5. Catch each worker's unwind with `catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(...))`. Advance candidate, capture completed banks/counters/rewards/record, assert done matches prediction. If done, replace with newly seeded candidate **before** preparing returned observation. `prepare` obtains one validated snapshot; infallible `write_env` fills two `ObsRowMut`s in unpublished staging. No live game/counter/record/output writes.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.4.md:547:The kernel already clones then commits only after successful `step_in_place` (W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:1443–1449): Result errors do **not** partially mutate its original Game. Outer clones still protect successful peers from another env's failure and later reset/encoder failure. Baseline costs **two game clones plus one prepare snapshot**. Measure separately, optimized, one live env, early and dense states:20 warmups, at most200 samples, watchdog <120s/<1GB including child build/run processes. Report clone/snapshot/validation/write costs/source/build/RSS in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.4/timing.json`. At128 env/rank Claude measures whole-update costs; reopen cheaper undo/staging only if clones are observed to dominate. No invented threshold, speed claim or kernel patch.
ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/briefs/1.4.md:553:Default `episodeSteps=720` finishes exactly at transition719 (W:engine_rs/src/lib.rs:701,1487); episodeSteps1/2 both finish after one transition. Use terminal_banks/snapshot.done, asserting the reservation prediction. Rewards/dones/transition banks/counters/records describe the completed game; observation banks/masks/clock describe the newly seeded game. Exactly one new seed per finished env. Raw-bank winner0/1/−1; record i64 episode_steps719, finite f64 bank_0/bank_1/margin_0 and `[32]` int64 econ_0/econ_1. Terminal records last until next successful step or selected explicit reset; failure preserves them.
